  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates an electrochemical setup that appears to generate deuterium ions from heavy water and drive them into a palladium target, where fusion is proposed to occur. In the upper part, an electrochemical cell contains D2O beneath an iridium anode and above a palladium cathode. A battery applies voltage across the cell, and a small reaction box shows deuterium-containing water being split so that negatively charged OD− remains in solution while positively charged deuterium species are produced at the cathode.\n\nBelow the cell, these D+ ions are shown as an ion beam moving upward into the palladium cathode. The enlarged circular inset gives the main scientific idea: deuterium ions enter the palladium lattice, diffuse through the metal, and accumulate in interstitial sites between palladium atoms. Arrows indicate their motion within the crystal. At one location labeled “Fusion,” two deuterium nuclei come together closely enough to react.\n\nOverall, the diagram presents a mechanism linking electrochemical loading of deuterium, ion acceleration into palladium, and possible fusion inside the metal lattice. It is a conceptual schematic rather than a quantitative plot.",
